Chapter: WasmPlugRing
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 The ring-fed wasm plug body: the same IR parser and the same WasmEmitter as
 `codex/plugs/wasm/WasmPlug.codex`, behind the compiler's own serial-ring
 intake instead of a serial file chardev. The ladder's `ZigPlugRing` is the
 crib and the reason is its: the ring is a machine-code loop that builds the
 Text one byte per byte and stops at NUL, and NUL is a byte no IR-CCE stream
 ever contains.

 IT DIFFERS FROM ZigPlugRing IN ITS FRAMING, and only there. The zig plug knows
 its output's length before it emits a byte, so it announces `SIZE:<n>` and the
 host reads exactly that many. This emitter cannot: `emit-wasm-chapter-stream`
 prints the module as it walks the IR, precisely so that a multi-megabyte text
 is never held at once against a bump allocator that never reclaims. So the end
 is marked instead of the beginning, and `ring_compile`'s `sentinel=` argument
 is the host half of that.

 The trailer is printed on the SAME channel as the module, which is why it must
 be a string no WAT can contain. `WASMRING-DONE` is not a wasm token and cannot
 appear inside a name, since `wat-sanitize` maps every Codex hyphen to an
 underscore before a name reaches the output.

Section: Body

  opening : [Console, FileSystem] Nothing = act
   mode-result <- read-line
   when mode-result
    is Just (mode) -> ring-wasm-emit mode
    is None -> print-line-uni "WASMRING-FAIL: no mode line"
  end

  ring-wasm-emit : Text -> [Console, FileSystem] Nothing
  ring-wasm-emit (mode) = act
   src <- read-serial-cce mode
   let parsed = parse-ir-chapter src
   in act
    emit-wasm-chapter-stream (parsed.chapter) (parsed.type-defs)
    print-line-uni ("WASMRING-DONE ir=" & show (text-length src) & " defs=" & show (list-length ((parsed.chapter).defs)))
   end
  end
